## When to invoke this skill

Adds something new to an app or website that has its code on this computer:
a page, a setting, a button that does something, an API route, a new field.
Use when asked to "build", "add", "implement" or "make it do". Blip's skill.
A bug in what exists is `/code-fix`; checking someone's change is
`/code-review`; getting it merged is `/code-ship`.

## The hard boundary

- **Read before writing.** Find out how this repo is built, run and tested
  before changing a line. Follow its rules: `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`,
  `CONTRIBUTING.md`, the README, the linters it already runs.
- **Its own branch, never main.** All work happens on a branch
  (`feat/<slug>`) or a worktree. Never commit to the main branch, never
  rewrite history that others have, never force-push.
- **Only what was asked.** Build the feature and what it needs — no
  drive-by refactors, upgrades, renames or restyling. Note anything else
  worth doing as a follow-up instead.
- **Match the code around it.** Same naming, structure, comment density and
  idioms as the files next to it. New dependencies only with a yes.
- **No secrets.** Never print, commit or paste keys, tokens or `.env`
  values. Never sign in anywhere for the person.
- **Nothing live.** No deploys, no pushes to shared branches, no database
  migrations against a real database, no changes on a server. That is
  `/code-ship`, after a yes.
- **"Done" means seen working.** Tests pass and you watched the feature work
  (in the browser for anything with a screen). Anything less is reported as
  "built, not verified", with what is missing.

## What you need first

- **What it should do** — the feature in a sentence, and what "done" looks
  like to the person (a page that shows X, a button that does Y).
- **Where the code is** — a folder or repo on this computer (clone it into
  the working folder if they give a URL).
- **What must not change** — files, behaviour, APIs others depend on.

If the request already says enough, ask nothing. Otherwise ask for what is
missing in a SINGLE message, then wait.

## Procedure

**1. Learn the repo.** Read the rules files, the README and the build/test
setup (`package.json` scripts, `Makefile`, `go.mod`, `pyproject.toml`…).
Run `git status` and note the branch and anything uncommitted — never mix
the person's unsaved work into yours; if there is some, ask before going on.
Find the files this feature touches and read them whole. Run the existing
tests once so you know the baseline.

Scratch for this run (logs, screenshots, notes) never goes into the repo:
put it in `$SCRATCH` when it is set, else a folder from `mktemp -d`. The
repo holds only the change, so `git status` shows nothing that isn't part
of it.

**2. Plan in small steps.** Write the plan in the reply: the files to change
or add, the tests to add, how you will see it working, and anything you had
to decide. Keep each step something you can check on its own. A plan that
changes a public API, the data model or a dependency waits for the person's
yes; a small, contained one goes ahead.

**3. Branch.** `git switch -c feat/<slug>` from the branch the person named
(default: the repo's main branch, up to date). For a long job alongside
other work, a worktree keeps the person's checkout untouched.

**4. Build it, step by step.** After each step, run the fastest check that
covers it (the type checker, the unit tests for that file). Fix what breaks
before the next step. Add tests for the new behaviour the way the repo
already writes tests — the happy path and the edge that would hurt most.

**5. See it working.** Run the full test suite and the linters the repo
uses. Start the app the way the README says and use the feature in the
browser: click it, fill it, reload, try the edge. Take a screenshot into
scratch. Any failure → back to step 4.

**6. Show the diff.** `git diff --stat` and the change itself: what each
file does now, in plain sentences. Then ask whether to commit.

**7. Commit — only with a yes.** One commit (or a few, one idea each) with a
message in the repo's style: a short summary line, then why. Do not push;
offer `/code-ship` to push the branch and open the pull request.

**8. Report.** What was built, the files changed, the tests added and
their result, what you watched work, and anything left as a follow-up — in
a few lines.

